<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Unsandboxed by necessity: the sandbox blocks the accessibility and window
         capture access this app is entirely built on, and the temporary exceptions
         that would allow it are not grantable for a utility like this. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key><false/>

    <!-- Required to load Sparkle.framework, and a real weakening — say so plainly.
         Library Validation restricts a process to loading code signed by the same Team
         ID. A self-signed certificate has no Team ID, so the check rejects even our own
         embedded framework, signed by our own identity. Sparkle documents this: without
         an Apple Developer ID there is no configuration in which both Sparkle and
         library validation work.

         What it costs: with this on, a library signed by anyone — or by nobody — can be
         loaded into a process that holds Screen Recording and Accessibility. The
         hardened runtime's other protections stay on, and the app remains unsandboxed
         either way, but this is strictly less safe than not having it.

         Remove this the day a Developer ID exists. It is the single clearest thing the
         $99 buys, and docs/RELEASE_PLAN.md says so. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
